linuxkpi: wake_up() wakes every waiter

Audit finding W5. linux_wake_up() stopped after nr successful wakeups, and wake_up()
passes 1. In linux nr limits exclusive waiters only; non-exclusive ones are all woken.
There are no exclusive waiters in this layer, so with two threads waiting on one queue
(two entities flushing on one scheduler) the second slept until its timeout.

--- a/src/Kernel/fbsdkpi/freebsd/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_schedule.c
+++ b/src/Kernel/fbsdkpi/freebsd/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_schedule.c
@@ -295,14 +295,18 @@
 
 	if (!locked)
 		spin_lock(&wqh->lock);
+	/*
+	 * linux's nr counts exclusive waiters only, and every non-exclusive
+	 * waiter is woken whatever nr is. This layer has no exclusive waiters
+	 * (no WQ_FLAG_EXCLUSIVE), so wake_up() must wake them all: stopping
+	 * after the first left a second waiter of the same queue asleep.
+	 */
+	(void)nr;
 	list_for_each_entry_safe(pos, next, &wqh->task_list, task_list) {
-		if (pos->func == NULL) {
-			if (wake_up_task(pos->private, state) != 0 && --nr == 0)
-				break;
-		} else {
-			if (pos->func(pos, state, 0, NULL) != 0 && --nr == 0)
-				break;
-		}
+		if (pos->func == NULL)
+			(void)wake_up_task(pos->private, state);
+		else
+			(void)pos->func(pos, state, 0, NULL);
 	}
 	if (!locked)
 		spin_unlock(&wqh->lock);

